  Instructions for running Phase 1
  ----------------------------------------------------------
  
  Step 1 — Start core infrastructure

  docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d \
    postgres redis pubsub-emulator litellm prometheus grafana

docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d postgres redis pubsub-emulator litellm prometheus grafana    
docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml --env-file .env up -d postgres redis pubsub-emulator litellm prometheus grafana
  Step 2 — Start Airflow

  docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d airflow-init
  # Wait ~30s then:
  docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d \
    airflow-webserver airflow-scheduler airflow-worker

  Step 3 — Run DB migrations

  DATABASE_URL="postgresql://infraops:admin@localhost:5432/infraops" \
    python infrastructure/scripts/migrate.py

  Step 4 — Set up PubSub topics and subscriptions

  This creates the infraops.provisioning.requests topic and infraops-provisioning-requests-vm-sub subscription on the local emulator — the DAG's PubSubPullSensor listens on that subscription.
  
  PUBSUB_EMULATOR_HOST=localhost:8085 \
  GCP_PROJECT_ID=local-dev \
    python infrastructure/pubsub/setup.py
  
  Step 5 — Start MCP servers and agents

  docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d \
    mcp-postgres mcp-pubsub mcp-gcp-resource mcp-backstage

  docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d \
    orchestrator-agent provisioning-agent

  docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d web-backend

  Step 6 — Create a test user

  DATABASE_URL="postgresql://infraops:changeme@localhost:5432/infraops" \
    python infrastructure/scripts/create_user.py \
      --user-id dev@yourorg.com \
      --role developer

  Save the printed API key.

  ---
  Step 7 — Submit a VM creation request

  curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/v1/requests \
    -H "X-API-Key: <your-api-key>" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"raw_input": "Create a VM named my-test-vm with 4 CPUs in us-central1"}' \
    | jq .
  
  The response will be 202 with a job_id and confirmation_summary. Read the summary — it lists what will be created and that you have 20 minutes to confirm.
  
  Step 8 — Confirm the provisioning
  
  curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/v1/jobs/<job_id>/confirm \
    -H "X-API-Key: <your-api-key>" \
    | jq .
  
  This publishes a ProvisioningRequestEvent to PubSub. The Airflow DAG's PubSubPullSensor picks it up within ~10 seconds.
  
  Step 9 — Monitor job status
  
  # Poll until status is succeeded or failed
  watch -n 5 'curl -s http://localhost:8000/api/v1/jobs/<job_id> \
    -H "X-API-Key: <your-api-key>" | jq ".status, .gcp_resource_id"'

  You can also watch the Airflow DAG run at http://localhost:8080 → DAGs → provision_vm_dag.

  ---
  What happens in Airflow

  wait_for_message → update_in_progress → dry_run_validate → provision_vm → register_backstage → succeeded
                                                                                ↓ (on failure)
                                                                           rollback_vm → failed

  One important caveat: the register_backstage step calls the real Backstage API (BACKSTAGE_API_URL). If Backstage isn't running locally, that step will fail and trigger rollback even though the VM was created
  in GCP. To avoid this for local testing, set BACKSTAGE_API_URL=http://localhost:7007/api and either run Backstage locally or set BACKSTAGE_API_TOKEN to a dummy value and accept the rollback — or skip the
  backstage step by commenting out that task in the DAG temporarily. 

